WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.379 --> 00:00:03.890
Hello everybody and welcome to today's webinar.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:09.750
Today we are talking about gene and protein,
chemical names and aliases, synonyms and other

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.750
confusing terms.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.830
Rana Morris is going to be giving the webinar.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.760
This is Peter Cooper.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:21.300
The directory for the slides and Q&amp;A document
will be on the FTP site.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.249
That go.usa compressed URL will take you there.

00:00:25.249 --> 00:00:30.999
I'm going to turn this over to Rana now and
she will talk about chemical and protein and

00:00:30.999 --> 00:00:32.800
gene names and aliases.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.090
Hello and welcome.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:40.230
This NCBI Minute is based on two Insights
blog posts on topics that many users have

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.380
asked us about previously.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:46.370
I will show you how to find those at the end
of the presentation.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.960
As science progresses what we call things
can change over time.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.260
Gene symbols, protein names, what we call
chemical compounds.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.670
This can be confusing not just when conversing
about scientific research but particularly

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:03.190
when looking for and at articles that have
been published over time.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:08.350
In this NCBI Minute I'll give a couple quick
examples about how the use of names and terms

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.350
can be confusing.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:15.200
I will show you how we deal with that issue
in house, where we put the information on

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.149
our webpage so that others can benefit from
our work, and show you some of the FTP files

00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:23.299
that you can download if you want to get this
information in bulk.

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:30.200
Two of the main groups that handle this nomenclature
issue here at NCBI deal with assigning terms

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.889
for genes and proteins and chemical compounds.

00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:39.820
A classic example of a problem with gene symbols
is shown here.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:47.509
I found a paper published in 1999 describing
the effect of a gene product called p21sdl1

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:49.990
in four different cancer cell lines.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:59.049
In 2010 another paper describes the gene p21Cip1
in a CML cancer study.

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:04.849
Two years later, p21Waf1 was examined for
it’s effect with Estrogen Receptor α in

00:02:04.849 --> 00:02:06.399
6 breast cancer cell lines.

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.819
These genes are actually all the same.

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:14.800
There are many papers that have actually concatenated
these terms in various combinations: Cip1/Waf1,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.470
Waf1/Sdl1, p21/Cip1.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:24.250
It’s not even really just use based on an
evolution of the term through time, since

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:30.060
there was a publication back in 1994 which
concatenated all of the various possibilities

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.600
together in one swell foop.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:40.820
Last year a publication came out describing
the effect of CDKN1A in thyroid cancer.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:41.820
Guess what?

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.390
It’s also the same gene.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:48.980
In fact, the Official Gene Symbol as designated
by the Human Gene Nomenclature Committee is

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:56.360
actually CDKN1A – which is the symbol for
the cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor 1A.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.460
All the other terms that have been used
are now known as aliases or synonyms or also

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:06.620
known as terms for the same gene and gene-product,
which in this case is a protein.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.150
A similar and, perhaps, more long-running
problem has occurred in the world of chemicals.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:16.320
Here’s the most accurate “name” for
a chemical – actually it is the 2D view

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.150
of the atomic structure.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:23.700
But early in it’s research life it was designated
with various code-terms.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:30.260
When the structure is known, it can have an
official nomenclature-designated (IUPAC) name

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.900
and there is often a registry identifier designated
for it (like CAS).

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:41.490
Over time, this was identified as an important
compound and given a more easily pronounceable

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.710
and marketable name, Tamoxifen.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:49.620
Although various companies decided to sell
forms of it under other names.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.620
What do you do with all of this?

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.960
How do you look it things up in a literature
search?

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.620
What do you search with if you want to find
something to buy?

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.120
So how does NCBI deal with this situation?

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.040
We’ll start with terms for Genes &amp; Proteins.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.160
We have a curation group at NCBI called RefSeq
– the Reference Sequence project.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.650
This is a group of curators who examine the
literature and work with collaborators in

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:18.650
the community along with automated data-gathering
processes to compile a list of terms that

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:21.630
have been used for a particular gene or protein.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:26.440
This includes an officially designated Gene
Symbol and protein name – when one exists,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.720
along with all of the other terms that have
been used for it in the literature,

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:36.090
in reputable databases such as UniProt, PDB
and SwissProt, and from submissions from Experts

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.430
in the field.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:43.810
We display compiled lists of all of these
on records in the Gene database.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:48.290
Gene Symbols are shown in the Summary section
at the top of the page, with the Official

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:54.510
Gene Symbols designated and others that have
been used listed in the Also known as section.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:58.510
Protein names are listed in the General Protein
Information, Names section,

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.590
with an Official name designated along with
others shown below.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:05.670
Here’s an example for the CDKN1A gene.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:10.590
Here’s the Gene page for CDKN1A (cyclin-dependent
kinase inhibitor 1A) - the human one.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:16.410
Just to remind you, there is a Table of Contents
on the right that lists all of the sections

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.500
of this record.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:22.251
In the top section is the Summary,
which lists the Official Gene Symbol and Protein

00:05:22.251 --> 00:05:23.251
name.

00:05:23.251 --> 00:05:27.470
The organization that made the decision is
listed right here.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:33.930
In this case, this was decided by the Human
Gene Nomenclature Committee – the HGNC.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:39.040
Other alias Gene Symbols that have been used
in history are listed in the Also Known As

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:40.080
section.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.610
Further down the page, you’ll find Protein
Names listed in the General Protein Information

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:51.610
section, under Names and Preferred Names.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:57.210
While the RefSeq Group deals with Genes &amp; Proteins,
we have another group that deals with Chemicals.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:02.490
The PubChem Group has automated processes
that aggregate all of those terms that have

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:05.290
been used for Chemical Compounds.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:11.290
Aliases or Synonyms for chemicals are compiled
from MeSH Indexing curators at NLM and from

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:14.330
submitters of chemical data to PubChem.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.919
In addition, you may remember from your old
organic chemistry class, that there are several

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.919
different nomenclatures used for chemicals
based on their atomic structure.

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:25.840
We have a software program that will actually
compute these terms based on the chemical

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.780
structure and these are called, Descriptors.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:33.580
These include our old friend (or nemesis),
IUPAC, as well as several others such as InChI,

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.590
InChI Key, SMILES and SMARTs.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:42.150
Finally, we aggregate Identifiers that are
used by various organizations such as FDA’s

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:47.570
UNII term, the EU’s European Chemical Agency’s
Number, and others.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:52.240
These are placed on PubChem Compound record
pages in a Names &amp; Identifiers section.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.290
Here’s an example for Tamoxifen.

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:58.820
Here’s the PubChem Compound page for Tamoxifen.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.169
The record has a top Summary section, which
is pretty helpful.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:08.300
PubChem puts it’s Table of Contents on the
left - listing all of its sections

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:11.590
(and they are expandable for a multi-level
outline view).

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:16.820
It’s the 3rd section that lists those Names
&amp; Identifiers that have been aggregated and

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:18.430
calculated.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:22.870
First come the Computed Descriptors, which
are generated from the chemical structure

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:26.139
–
IUPAC, InChI, InChI Key and so on….

00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:31.290
Then below that is the Other Identifiers list
including a CAS Registry number (when we have

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:38.050
it), an EC Number and the FDA’s UNII, for
example.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:44.670
Then, the Synonyms section includes those
compiled by MeSH Curators at the NLM

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:51.000
and a list collected from all of the submitter's
of information to PubChem.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:56.520
Note that you can DOWNLOAD the information
in this record at the top of the page.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.070
You can also do this back on the GENE record
page.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:03.090
But what about if you want to download, not
just one Gene or Chemical’s information,

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:07.930
but a whole bunch of it all at once?

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:09.790
Back to Genes &amp; Proteins.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:15.150
In addition to displaying those also known
as and names directly on the Gene record pages,

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:21.930
the RefSeq Group also produces several files
that they regularly update on the NCBI FTP

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:23.300
site.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:27.990
These are tab-separated text files containing
lots and lots of information.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:32.070
These include Gene Symbols and Protein Names
among a lot of other cool stuff.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.529
These are called GENE_INFO files.

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:41.570
Here’s the full path to find the Gene_Info
files on our FTP site.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:50.570
To help visualize this, they are located in
the Gene database folder, in the DATA folder,

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.670
in a GENE_INFO subfolder.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:09:01.370
You can get the ALL_Data.gene_info.gz file
which provides lots of information for ALL

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.260
the organisms listed in the Gene database.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:09.930
Alternatively, you can get an organism-specific
file, which is much smaller and is, frankly,

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:11.320
what most people want.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:18.390
For example, you can go into the Mammalia
subfolder and then select the Homo_sapiens.gene_info.gz

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.980
file to get only the information about Human
genes.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:26.610
Note that we also have files specifically
for Mouse, Rat, Dog, etc.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:31.790
Also, please note that these are pretty large
G-Zipped (compressed) files.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:34.910
What do these files look like?

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:38.910
Well they are made up of 15 tab-separated
columns with lots of information.

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:43.890
I’ve turned the table over 90-degrees and
have shown only a few columns here, because

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.270
I think it make it easier for you to see what
we have.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:49.880
Here’s an example of the data pertaining
to

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:56.190
the CDKN1A gene or cyclin-dependent kinase
inhibitor 1A that I showed you earlier, showing:

00:09:56.190 --> 00:10:03.459
the Gene database identifier (GeneID), correlated
Gene Symbols and then the names designated

00:10:03.459 --> 00:10:06.320
for that particular gene product.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.940
Please note that the organism-specific ones
are easily importable into Excel or parseable

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:15.160
with even much more helpful data about the
genes I'm not showing here.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:22.870
And you can keep, discard or reorganize the
columns however you want.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:26.399
While gene and protein information is the
charge of the RefSeq Group, remember that

00:10:26.399 --> 00:10:32.240
chemical information is gathered by the PubChem
Group and displayed on PubChem Compound records.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:37.990
The PubChem Group also produces helpful FTP
files which are, perhaps, even easier to use

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:40.620
to correlate Synonyms for a Chemical Compound.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:46.959
There is a single file on the NCBI FTP site
which has only 2 tab-separated columns –

00:10:46.959 --> 00:10:51.980
with the PubChem Compound Identifier (or CID)
in the first column and then a corresponding

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:53.180
Synonym in the second.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:58.360
However, if you are interested in those other
terms used for chemicals – Computed Descriptors

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:01.420
and Identifiers –
you’ll need to download the full PubChem

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:04.230
Compound database record file to get all of
those.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.020
So where are they?

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:11.520
Here’s the full path to find the PubChem
files on our FTP site.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:19.190
To help visualize this, they are located in
the PubChem database folder, in the Compound

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:25.089
folder, and then the Extras subfolder –
there are a lot of really nice and helpful

00:11:25.089 --> 00:11:26.220
files.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:34.060
You can access the CID-Synonym-filtered.gz
file, down here at the bottom, to get a cleaned

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:38.850
list of all of the Synonyms provided to PubChem
for chemical compounds.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.339
The G-Zipped (compressed) file is pretty big
(almost a Gigabyte), but it is a simple file

00:11:43.339 --> 00:11:49.570
containing only 2 columns: the CID &amp; the Synonym.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:54.940
There are other files in this Extras folder
that may be of interest, such as the CID-MeSH

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:59.180
file which shows only the single MeSH-preferred
term for the chemical

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:04.649
(which we use for the name at the very top
of PubChem Compound records).

00:12:04.649 --> 00:12:08.270
For all of those other Computed Descriptors
and Identifiers, you’ll need to go to the

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:11.360
Compound CURRENT-Full record files.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.330
They are produced in several formats, so you
can get whichever one is best for you: ASN.1,

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.250
XML or SDF.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:24.240
Here’s an example of a portion of the Tamoxifen
data in the SDF-formatted file.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:30.020
Embedded in the data are IUPAC Names, INCHI
&amp; INCHI KEY terms, SMILES terms and lots of

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:31.380
other data.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:36.190
Now, you don’t have to remember all of this
or have to watch this video again.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:41.410
ALL of this information has already been written
up with descriptions and links in two NCBI

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:44.310
Insights Blog posts.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:49.589
The NCBI Insights Blog provides information
about NCBI resources with tips &amp; tricks to

00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:52.360
help you use our site and make cool discoveries.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:58.579
One NCBI Insights Blog post fully explains
how to get the Also known As terms for Genes

00:12:58.579 --> 00:13:00.220
&amp; Proteins.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:05.040
Another talks about how to get Chemical Synonyms
(and even mentions simple APIs that you can

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:09.060
use, if you prefer those to downloading large
files).

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:11.860
I’ve included bit.ly links here to make
them easy to find.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.920
But you can always check in on
or search the NCBI Insights Blog site for

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:18.700
more cool tips &amp; tricks.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:22.940
Many helpful How to videos are - and this
one soon will be –

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:25.029
available on our YouTube channel.

00:13:25.029 --> 00:13:29.650
And, as always, if you have any questions
about or need help with our resources –

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:35.820
send an Email to our HelpDesk at info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov
.

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:36.840
Thanks for your attention.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:38.960
[Peter Cooper] Thanks Rana.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:40.980
We have time for a few questions.

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:45.280
I see -- I answered one in the question pod
that I think everybody has seen.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:49.930
The other one was about where you can get
a copy of the slide deck.

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:57.480
Well, that [sic] bit.ly (go.usa.gov) link
on the cover slide has that and I will put

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:03.141
that in the question pod in a minute.

00:14:03.141 --> 00:14:05.110
Okay, we have one more question in the question
pod.

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:09.520
How often do the gene and protein databases
update the synonyms and identifiers?

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.710
[Rana Morris] I'm not sure about the gene,
I'll have to get back to you on that.

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:17.630
PubChem updates those files every day.

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:23.750
When new information comes in it takes a couple
days to process and then that file is updated,

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:27.019
I think, at 7 PM every night.

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:28.140
That is constantly updated.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:34.060
I believe the gene record is updated a little
less frequently but still pretty well and

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.180
we can find that information out.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:40.390
Send an email to info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and
we will get that information to you.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:47.010
[Peter Cooper] I sent out the compressed URL
to that FTP site that has the slide deck,

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:49.110
to everyone.

00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:52.920
Another listener, from NCBI, has chimed in.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.510
The GENE_INFO file on the FTP site seems to
be updated daily as well.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:03.269
